title: 'pallet-revive: cold/hot pricing for calls, transfers, code loads and contract creation,
  and limits for transfer and send receivers'
doc:
- audience: [Runtime Dev, Runtime User]
  description: |-
    The cold/hot pricing introduced in #12104 now also covers the state items accessed by the call
    opcodes (EVM `CALL`, `STATICCALL`, `DELEGATECALL` and the PVM call and delegate-call host
    functions), a call's value transfer, code loads and contract creation, following EIP-2929. An
    item is hot if it was accessed earlier in the same transaction and no revert rolled that
    access back. Each operation is charged the cold benchmark if any of the items it accesses is
    cold, and the hot benchmark if all are hot.

    The callee of a call shaped like Solidity's `transfer()` or `send()` (an EVM `CALL`, or a
    PolkaVM `call_evm` without the `READ_ONLY` flag, that moves value with a gas limit of 0 or
    moves no value with a gas limit of exactly 2300), and every contract it calls, can no longer
    write persistent contract storage, move value, or create or terminate a contract. On Ethereum,
    the 2300-gas stipend such a call gives its callee does not pay for these operations.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `WeightInfo` has 15 new required methods: `code_load`, `chained_delegation_check`,
    `chained_delegation_check_hot`, `seal_call_hot`, `seal_call_transfer_hot`,
    `seal_delegate_call_hot`, `call_with_pvm_code_per_byte_hot`,
    `call_with_evm_code_per_byte_hot`, `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade`,
    `access_list_touch_hot_address_upgrade`, `access_list_touch_cold_address_empty`,
    `access_list_touch_cold_address_full`, `access_list_touch_hot_address_single_element`,
    `access_list_touch_hot_address_full` and `access_list_rollback_address_amortization`.
    Some existing benchmarks also measure different work now, so runtimes with their own
    `pallet_revive` weights must regenerate them:
    - `call`, `eth_call`, `seal_call`, `seal_call_transfer`, `seal_call_transfer_to_existing`,
      `seal_delegate_call`, `instantiate`, `seal_instantiate` and `seal_instantiate_transfer` no
      longer measure the reads of the contract blob and its code info, and `instantiate`,
      `seal_instantiate` and `seal_instantiate_transfer` no longer measure the write that bumps
      the code's reference count. These are now charged with the `code_load` weight when the code
      info or the code blob is cold.
    - `seal_instantiate`, `seal_instantiate_transfer`, `evm_instantiate` and
      `evm_instantiate_transfer` also measure a read of the new address's `OriginalAccount` entry.
    - `seal_call_transfer` and `seal_call_transfer_to_existing` also measure checking whether the
      callee has a `frame_system` account.
    - `access_list_touch_hot_full` now reads an entry that was already read, so it no longer
      measures upgrading an entry from a read to a write; `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade` measures
      that.
    - Since the access list grew from 2,048 to 4,096 entries, the benchmarks that fill it now go
      up to 4,096.

    Code outside the pallet that uses `pallet_revive::precompiles`:
    - `Ext` no longer has `touch_storage_access` and `peek_storage_access`, and
      `StorageAccessKind::Persistent` no longer holds a plain `Warmth`, so the closure that
      `StorageAccessKind::new` takes no longer returns one either. Code that charged a contract
      storage access should charge the cold price with the new
      `StorageAccessKind::new_persistent_cold`, or use `StorageAccessKind::Transient` for transient
      storage, for example:
      ```rust
      env.frame_meter_mut().charge_weight_token(RuntimeCosts::GetStorage {
          len,
          kind: StorageAccessKind::new_persistent_cold(),
      })?;
      ```
    - `StorageOp` and `Warmth` are no longer re-exported.
    - In `RuntimeCosts`, `DelegateCallBase` is removed, `CallBase` now holds the warmth of the
      call's entries, `CallTransferSurcharge` replaces `dust_transfer` with the transfer's warmth
      and whether the callee has a `frame_system` account (a transfer to a precompile keeps only
      the `dust` flag), and `ChainedDelegationCheck` and `AccessListRegistration` are new. Code
      that charged `CallBase`, `DelegateCallBase` or `CallTransferSurcharge` can no longer build
      these variants; it should charge the cold price from `WeightInfo` instead, for example
      `env.charge(T::WeightInfo::seal_call(0))`, `seal_delegate_call()` for a delegate call, or,
      for a call with value, `seal_call_transfer(dust).max(seal_call_transfer_to_existing(dust))`
      in place of both `CallBase` and `CallTransferSurcharge`.
    - `Ext::call` takes a `CallProtections` argument in place of its reentrancy protection and
      `read_only` arguments. The struct holds the reentrancy protection, `read_only`, and
      `stipend_limited`, which is set for calls shaped like a `transfer()` or `send()` and for
      delegate calls with a gas limit of exactly 2300. A precompile that passed
      `ReentrancyProtection::AllowReentry` and `false` now passes `CallProtections::none()`. A
      precompile that used another reentrancy level should set it in the `reentrancy` field, and
      one that made a read-only call should set the `read_only` field, for example:
      ```rust
      // Before
      env.call(&resources, &to, value, input, ReentrancyProtection::Strict, true)?;
      // After
      let protections = CallProtections {
          reentrancy: ReentrancyProtection::Strict,
          read_only: true,
          stipend_limited: false,
      };
      env.call(&resources, &to, value, input, protections)?;
      ```
    - `ReentrancyProtection::AllowNext` is removed and replaced by `stipend_limited`.
      `ReentrancyProtection` is also exported as `pallet_revive::ReentrancyProtection`, so code
      that imports it from there is affected too.
    - In a stipend-limited frame, `Ext::set_storage`, `Ext::terminate_caller`,
      `ExtWithInfo::instantiate` and a value transfer through `Ext::call` return `OutOfGas`. In a
      read-only frame, they return `StateChangeDenied` instead, and so do
      `Ext::set_transient_storage` and a value transfer through `Ext::call` with `read_only` set.
      Before, the caller had to check the read-only flag; now these functions enforce it. A
      precompile that changes state by calling into another pallet, for example to move balances
      or assets, must call the new `Ext::ensure_not_stipend_limited` first. The assets,
      asset-conversion, XCM and vesting precompiles do so.

    `ContractBlob::store_code` now returns `Result<Option<BalanceOf<T>>, DispatchError>`. On
    success it holds the deposit collected for newly stored code, or `None` when the code was
    already stored.

    `ExecConfig` has a new field, `first_frame_reads_paid`, which `with_first_frame_reads_paid`
    sets. Setting the field adds the called contract's entries to the access list, and the
    transfer's when the call moves value, so later accesses to them are charged hot. Code that
    calls `bare_call` directly should set it only when the weight of the extrinsic making the
    call already covers these reads, as the weights of the `call` and `eth_call` dispatchables
    do. A dry run must set it exactly when the dispatch it estimates does, or its estimate can be
    too low; the runtime APIs' dry runs of `call` and `eth_call` set it. Code that builds an
    `ExecConfig` by listing all its fields, instead of calling a constructor such as
    `ExecConfig::new_substrate_tx()`, must add `first_frame_reads_paid`.

    `pallet_revive::runtime_api` has a new public function, `dry_run_call`. It performs a dry run
    of the `call` dispatchable, the same way the `call` runtime API does.

    The access list holds up to 4,096 entries per transaction (was 2,048), and the memory budgeted
    for its worst case grows from 1.5 MiB to 4 MiB. `integrity_test` checks that this memory,
    together with the rest of the memory the pallet requires, fits in half of `RuntimeMemory`, so
    a runtime close to that limit can now fail this check.

    `integrity_test` also checks that the storage a block can write fits in half of the difference
    between `PVFMemory` and `RuntimeMemory`, the memory a validator has beyond the block builder's.
    Before, it counted contract storage writes as close to zero, so they could not make that check
    fail. It now counts how many writes fit the block weight, each priced as a cold write without
    rollback, and multiplies that count by the memory each write uses.

    No storage migration is needed.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A call or delegate call to an EIP-7702 delegated account whose delegation target is not a
    deployed contract now also pays for reading the target's account info, which tells whether
    the target is itself delegated.

    A call with value pays for creating the callee's `frame_system` account only when that
    account does not exist, regardless of whether the transfer's state items are cold or hot.
    Before, every call with value paid for creating it. A call with value to a precompile still
    always pays for creating the precompile's `frame_system` account.

    A contract creation now sets aside the worst-case weight of registering the new contract in the
    access list before the constructor runs, so the constructor gets that much less weight. When
    the creation ends, the caller gets back the part of that weight the registration did not use,
    or all of it if the creation failed or reverted, even when the constructor used up all of its
    own weight.

    The `call` and `instantiate` dispatchables used to include the reads of the contract's code
    info and code blob in their own weight, and charged only the cost per byte of code to their
    `weight_limit`. Now those reads are charged to the `weight_limit` too, with the weight of the
    `code_load` benchmark, so a limit that was just enough before can be too low and should be
    estimated again with a dry run.

    A contract called through Solidity's `transfer()` or `send()` gets a stipend of 2300 gas. On
    Ethereum, the stipend cannot pay for a storage write, a value transfer, or creating or
    terminating a contract. In revive, only the stipend's weight limited these operations.
    The pallet now limits the callee to what the stipend pays for on Ethereum, for every call with
    that shape: an EVM `CALL`, or a PolkaVM `call_evm` without `READ_ONLY`, that moves value with a
    gas limit of 0, or moves no value with a gas limit of exactly 2300. In the callee, and in every
    contract it calls, these operations fail with `OutOfGas`:
    - persistent contract storage writes (`SSTORE`, the PolkaVM storage host functions, and
      `clearStorage` and `takeStorage` of the storage precompile),
    - value transfers,
    - contract creation (`CREATE`, `CREATE2`, PolkaVM `instantiate`),
    - contract termination (`SELFDESTRUCT`, PolkaVM `terminate`, `terminate` of the system
      precompile),
    - calls to a state-changing function of the assets, asset-conversion, XCM or vesting
      precompile.

    The contract that attempts such an operation halts on the EVM or traps on PolkaVM, and its
    caller sees a failed call. On Ethereum, the operation runs out of gas, with the same result.
    When a precompile denies such an operation, only the call to the precompile fails. Persistent
    contract storage reads, events, and transient storage reads and writes are not denied, but the
    stipend's weight must still pay for them. Calls with any other explicit gas limit are not
    affected, even one below 2300.

    A delegate call (an EVM `DELEGATECALL` or a PolkaVM `delegate_call_evm`) with a gas limit of
    exactly 2300 now gets the stipend and the same limits. Before, such a delegate call got the
    stipend but not the limits on the EVM, and neither on PolkaVM.

    These limits can break deployed contracts that perform one of the listed operations in the
    callee of a call shaped like a `transfer()` or `send()`, for example a `receive()` that writes
    storage, or in a delegate call with a gas limit of exactly 2300.

    The receiver may call back into the sender, as on Ethereum, under the same limits. Before, the
    EVM denied this after a zero-value `transfer()` or `send()` and in the callee of a `STATICCALL`
    with a gas limit of 2300: the reentrant call returned 0 in an EVM receiver, and a PolkaVM
    receiver trapped. A call from the receiver to a contract whose items are all hot, such as the
    sender contract, is now charged the hot benchmark, so the stipend can pay for some calls that
    ran out of gas before. Whether it pays for a given call depends on the runtime's weights, on
    how much of the stipend the receiver uses before the call, and on the size of the called
    contract's code.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-asset-conversion-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-xcm-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-vesting-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
